---
import Divider from "../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/foce/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Bonus Project: RGB Color Mixing</h1>

        <h4>
          Difficulty&nbsp;&nbsp;
          <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
        </h4>

        <h4 class="text-sn-theme">Optional &mdash; Extra Credit</h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>What This Is</h2>

          <p>
            One LED, three colors inside it, and every shade in between. Mix them
            with three knobs, or cycle them in code.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is the shortest project on the list and the one that explains
            something the rest of the unit never gets to: how a screen makes
            colors. Three channels, and brightness is just PWM.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="needs">
          <h2>What You&#39;ll Need</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>The tricolor RGB LED, with a resistor on <i>each</i> of its three color legs</li>
            <li>Three free GPIO pins &mdash; any three you are not using</li>
            <li>Optional: the potentiometer, if you want to mix by hand</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            An RGB LED is really three LEDs sharing one package and one common
            leg. Find out whether yours is <b>common cathode</b> (the shared leg
            goes to <code>GND</code>) or <b>common anode</b> (it goes to{" "}
            <code>3V3</code>) before you wire it &mdash; on a common anode part,
            a higher duty makes it <i>dimmer</i>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="tasks">
          <h2>Build It</h2>

          <ol>
            <li><b>Light one color at a time.</b> Red on its own, then green, then blue. Confirm which leg is which.</li>
            <li><b>Dim one channel</b> with <code>PWM</code> duty, the same way you set motor power in Activity 2.12.</li>
            <li><b>Write <code>set_color(r, g, b)</code></b> taking three brightness values, so the rest of your program thinks in colors rather than pins.</li>
            <li><b>Make the secondary colors.</b> Red plus green is yellow, green plus blue is cyan, all three is white. Check them against a screen if you like.</li>
            <li><b>Fade between two colors</b> smoothly, rather than jumping.</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="extend">
          <h2>Then Make It Yours</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>Use the knob to sweep through the rainbow</li>
            <li>Show temperature as a color instead of a bar &mdash; blue when cool, red when warm</li>
            <li>Fade the onboard RGB LED on GPIO 3 the same way and compare</li>
            <li>Use it as the rover&#39;s team color in the Sumo tournament</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Commence Project
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
